{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And the land shall be subdued.</b> One must know why the statement ״עַד הוֹרִישׁוֹ״ that he said just before was not sufficient. Further, one must know why it was necessary to say <b>and afterward you shall return</b>, for it is self-understood from his saying ״עַד הוֹרִישׁוֹ״ etc. — that is the boundary of the advance, and it is self-understood that they would return afterward. However, Moses' intention in this is to assure them of the good that flows from fulfilling the matter, for through the performance of the preceding deed they are assured of several assurances: (a) <b>and the land shall be subdued</b> — meaning, the land will surely be subdued before HaShem; (b) <b>and afterward you shall return</b> — meaning, He who spoke to them guaranteed that they would not suffer loss during all the time they would be in the land in the great wars, and that they would return whole to their homes. And although we find (Bava Batra 121a) that Yair son of Menasheh was killed in the war of Ai, half the tribe of Menasheh was not among those whom Moses addressed at that time, as is self-understood from the verses." "<b>Further,</b> He informed them that through this they would be clear before HaShem and before Israel — meaning: before HaShem — when they go out to war in the manner I have written, that their intention will be to sanctify the Name of Heaven and not for their own benefit in order to maintain the condition of the land; and before Israel — lest they be suspected of harboring slander in their hearts like the spies, as Moses said to them: ״You have risen up in place of your fathers, a brood of sinful men״ — that their intention was to turn back from following HaShem. And His saying <b>and the land shall be</b> etc. — He assured them that through this the land would be established in their hand as an inheritance and would not be torn from them; and from the implication of the matter you learn that if they do not act according to the word of Moses, all the good assurances will be stripped from them." "And His saying <b>before HaShem</b> — it is possible that the intention is to establish for them the sanctity of the land on the very grounds that they themselves stated at the beginning of their words. And this is the measure of his words: <b>and this land shall be</b> etc. — although it is outside the Land, it shall be considered for you <b>an inheritance before HaShem</b> — meaning, it shall be considered this inheritance which is before HaShem, and this is its sanctity, for according to this reasoning the land is considered holy — because it is before HaShem with no strip of profanity separating between it and HaShem who dwells in the Capital."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:16:51.829120144Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}